  "summary": "Recent bioinformatics papers and preprints extend structure-aware deep learning to antibody design, binding-site detection, and molecule generation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is conditioning learned models on protein or molecular structure and on pretrained representations, applied to antibody design, binding-site localization, drug-target interaction, and molecule generation. Several items address conformational heterogeneity directly: SIMORGH uses an SE(3)-equivariant, ensemble-aware approach to predict apo-state and cryptic ligand binding sites, and the pocket contrastive framework fuses reference conformations to improve robustness under receptor conformational variation. Other items emphasize training strategy, including SimPO preference optimization that updates only the decoder of frozen ESM-IF for antibody affinity, expectation-maximization for sequence-only paratope prediction, and conformal prediction giving sample-specific intervals for drug-target affinity. Coverage is heterogeneous, so the common element is methodological rather than a single target class.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
